# Branch reuse first

## Pyramid Layer

- Layer: L0 core rule.

## Use This When

- Before cutting a branch for any feature, fix or chore.
- When a bug is found on `main`.
- Before an operation that touches branches other than the one of the work at hand.

## Stop Here If

- You know which branch the work goes on and how it was brought up to date with `main`. Landing it is `/.cursor/rules/release-approval-flow.mdc`.

## The Rule

1. **An area keeps its branch.** An area is the responsibility a branch was
   opened for; its name and its card decide what that is. The path history of
   `main` only proposes candidates —
   `git log main --format='%h %(trailers:key=Squashed-from,valueonly)' -- <path>`
   names the branches that landed there. When it is unclear which branch an
   area belongs to, ask.

2. **Before cutting a branch, look for the area's branch, and resume it.**
   - First see what it carries that never landed:
     `git diff --stat main...<branch>` (three dots: the changes since its last
     merged-back landing; empty means nothing unlanded). `git log
     main..<branch>` is no use for this, because a squash leaves the branch's
     own commits outside `main` forever. For a branch whose landing was never
     merged back, use the tree check of 4 instead.
   - Another card's work under way there: ask. Unfinished or abandoned work:
     the owner's call; it is never squashed in unseen.
   - Bring it up to date by merging the latest `main` into it. Never rebase a
     branch that was pushed.
   - Only an area no branch carried gets a new branch, cut from `main`.

3. **A bug found on `main` is fixed in the branch that brought the code.**
   Find it through the `Squashed-from:` trailer of the squash that introduced
   the code (`git log -- <file>`), resume it as in 2, fix it with its test, and
   land it again. A new fix branch is cut from `main` only when no source
   branch exists, and the owner is told.

4. **A branch whose landing was never merged back** shares only its old fork
   point with `main`, so `git merge main` conflicts on every file the branch
   touched that `main` changed again after the squash. When its tip carries
   nothing unlanded — its tree equals the tree of its squash on `main`, the
   commit its `Squashed-from:` trailer names — sync it exactly:

   ```
   B=<branch>; S=<its squash on main>; OLD=$(git rev-parse "$B")
   test -z "$(git status --porcelain)" \
     && test "$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)" != "$B" \
     && test "$(git rev-parse "$OLD^{tree}")" = "$(git rev-parse "$S^{tree}")" \
     && git merge-base --is-ancestor "$S" main \
     && M=$(git commit-tree "main^{tree}" -p "$OLD" -p main -m "chore: sync $B with main") \
     && git update-ref "refs/heads/$B" "$M" "$OLD" \
     && git switch "$B"
   ```

   Run it as one chain, from a clean checkout that is NOT on the branch:
   `update-ref` moves the ref only, so moving a checked-out branch would
   leave its index and files at the old tree, and the next commit would
   quietly undo what `main` brought. Switching to it afterwards lets git
   materialise the new tree. The result is exact by construction and keeps
   both parents. When its tip does carry unlanded work, stop and ask. A
   branch whose landing was merged back needs none of this: `main` is
   already its ancestor, so `main` merges in the ordinary way, with the
   ordinary conflicts where both sides changed the same lines.

5. **Work that builds on another branch's unlanded work waits** for that
   branch to land, then merges `main` in.

6. **Experiments go on `experiment/<area>`**, cut from the branch whose area
   they explore. It is the one branch that may span several areas and carry
   its parent's unlanded work, and it never lands on `main` itself.
   - Every commit on it can be cherry-picked on its own: one destination
     branch, one kind of change, built only on commits going to the same
     branch or to one that lands earlier.
   - When it ends: if every commit is kept and belongs to the parent, it
     merges into the parent; otherwise its kept commits are cherry-picked onto
     the branches they belong to. Then it is deleted — its tip tagged
     `archive/<area>` first if notes cite its hashes.

7. **Touch only the branches of the work at hand.**
   - A branch rule applies from the day it is written. Branches that landed
     before it stay as they are: no retrofitted merge-backs. When such an area
     resumes, it is synced as in 4.
   - An operation over other branches is listed and proposed, never run as a
     sweep.
   - A destructive operation over several refs (reset, delete, rewrite) first
     prints each ref with its current tip and its target, then runs on exactly
     that list. A pushed branch is rewritten only on the owner's word.

## Why

A squash collapses a branch's history into one commit on `main`, so the branch
is the only carrier of how the work got there. Resuming the area's branch keeps
that history in one place instead of scattering it over micro-branches, and
merging each landing back keeps `main` an ancestor of the branch, so the next
round merges against `main`'s history instead of against a stale fork point.
